Your project
Teams of 3–5. One question, answered with a model you build yourselves.
A good project has

a question in one sentence, ending with a question mark;
a reference case: the model without your new ingredient;
a prediction, written down before you run anything;
one main figure that answers the question.


Graded on the final presentation and a written report with code that reproduces every figure.

Learning outcomes
By the end of the course you can

write down a compartment model, find its equilibria and compute \(R_0\);
explain why deterministic and stochastic models of the same process can disagree, and when that matters;
model the emergence of drug resistance and of new pathogen strains;
estimate a time-varying reproduction number from incidence data and judge how much to trust it;
turn a vague biological question into a model, a reference case and a testable prediction;
present a modelling result clearly in 10 minutes.
